{"availability":"available","full":"jimd1989/bitter","name":"bitter","description":"read-only bandwidth-friendly frontend to Twitter tweets","category":"Other","language":"Go","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2019-03-25T03:59:40Z","updated":"2019-04-03T07:46:58Z","license":"BSD-2-Clause","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["bandwidth-saver","read-only","twitter","webapp"],"demo":"https://dalrym.pl/bitter","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.377Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.377Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.377Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.377Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Go"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["I don't like Twitter; it's over-engineered and everybody there is really unpleasant. It would be easy enough to ignore if it weren't so essential. Somewhere along the line journalists stopped writing actual articles and started stringing tweets together instead. All roads lead to Twitter if you want to keep on top of current events.","I wrote bitter (the Kiwi pronunciation of \"better\") to make the process of reading a tweet you've been linked to as painless as possible. It's an API with a simple web frontend that returns tweets (and attached media) as HTML, JSON, or plaintext. It's a lot easier on your bandwidth and strips away all \"engagement\" options such as liking, retweeting, or even seeing who responded to the tweet. It gets you in and out of the Twitterverse with your sanity intact.","Of course the ultimate solution is to simply stop caring about the news, but I'm not quite there yet."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jimd1989/bitter/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"d37c3957908dfa857be3788dffbdc0a6940df88581027e168aaa7b784768cb85","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.540Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T13:40:54.540Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"I don't like Twitter; it's over-engineered and everybody ther